{#
  Site nav panel — the site, covered, with the way around it on top.

  Opened by the header's burger (nav-panel.js). Rendered once per page from
  _layouts/page.twig, next to the contact panel, and for the same reasons: a native
  <dialog> is the site's idiom for this.

  It is FIXED and full-bleed, over everything — the page gutter included. The header
  stays above it (z-index xl vs this panel's lg) so the logo and the close button
  survive, which is also why nav-panel.js opens it with show() rather than
  showModal(): a modal dialog's top layer would bury them.

  Links are queried, not hardcoded, exactly as the footer's quick links are: a single
  that isn't live takes its own link away rather than leaving one pointing at a page
  that no longer resolves. Case Studies is the exception — a section index with no
  entry of its own, so it's a route.
#}
{% set about = craft.entries().section('about').status('live').one() %}
{% set notesIndex = craft.entries().section('note').status('live').one() %}

{# One list, built first, so the active test below is written once rather than four
   times. `uri` is the entry's own URI, which is exactly the shape getPathInfo()
   returns — no leading slash, no host — so the two compare directly. Home is ''
   because the homepage single's URI is Craft's `__home__` sentinel.

   `owns` lists the path roots an item counts as its own, for the pages that sit
   UNDER a section rather than at it. Case Studies needs two because the index and
   the entries are different sections with different URIs:

     case-studies     the `caseStudy` SINGLE — the index page
     case-study/…     the `caseStudies` STRUCTURE — the studies themselves

   Notes needs only its own root: `notes/{slug}` covers the entries, `notes/<year>`
   the archive route, and the topics category group is also `notes/{slug}`, so all
   three are already under it. Home lists nothing — with a uri of '' a prefix test
   would claim every page on the site. #}
{# `key` is the access key. Set per item rather than taken from the first letter of
   the label, which would collide the day a second item starts with the same one — and
   would move on its own if a single's title were edited in the CMS. #}
{% set items = [{ label: 'Home', url: siteUrl(''), uri: '', owns: [], key: 'h' }] %}
{% if about %}
    {% set items = items|merge([{ label: about.title, url: about.url, uri: about.uri, owns: [about.uri], key: 'a' }]) %}
{% endif %}
{% set items = items|merge([{
    label: 'Case Studies',
    url: siteUrl('case-studies'),
    uri: 'case-studies',
    owns: ['case-studies', 'case-study'],
    key: 'c',
}]) %}
{% if notesIndex %}
    {% set items = items|merge([{ label: notesIndex.title, url: notesIndex.url, uri: notesIndex.uri, owns: [notesIndex.uri], key: 'n' }]) %}
{% endif %}

{# Contact, PHONES ONLY — the one item that isn't a plain page in this list.
   Above md the header carries it as a button; below md that button is hidden
   (.b-header__contact in _header.scss), which left the footer as the only way to it,
   so it comes back here instead. `mobileOnly` is what the stylesheet keys off.

   LAST on purpose, and not only because it reads that way: the stagger delays are
   nth-child, and an item hidden with display:none still counts for nth-child — put
   anywhere else it would leave a gap in the cascade above md.

   A route, like Case Studies, rather than a queried entry: contact-panel.js turns any
   same-tab click on a /contact link into the side panel, so this never navigates. It
   opens the panel OVER the open nav, which is deliberate — the nav closing behind the
   contact panel was reported as a bug and fixed by removing the click-outside. #}
{# 'c' belongs to Case Studies, so Contact takes the next free letter in its own
   word. Hoisted out of the hash below rather than annotated in place: a Twig comment
   inside a hash or array literal is a syntax error and 500s the page. #}
{% set contactKey = 't' %}
{% set items = items|merge([{
    label: 'Contact',
    url: siteUrl('contact'),
    uri: 'contact',
    owns: ['contact'],
    key: contactKey,
    mobileOnly: true,
}]) %}

{% set currentPath = craft.app.request.getPathInfo()|trim('/') %}

<dialog class="c-nav-panel" data-nav-panel aria-label="Site menu">
    {# tabindex -1 so a pointer-opened panel has somewhere silent to put focus —
       the same trick the contact panel uses. #}
    <nav class="c-nav-panel__inner" tabindex="-1">
        {# The site's own wrap, nested inside the panel's rhythm-500 inset, because
           that is exactly the pair of insets the logo sits behind: `body > .b-wrap`
           pads the slab off the viewport, and the header's own .b-wrap pads the logo
           off the slab. Reproducing the structure keeps the list's left edge on the
           logo's at every breakpoint, where a hardcoded padding would drift. #}
        <div class="b-wrap">
            <ul class="c-nav-panel__items">
                {% for item in items %}
                    {# Exact match on the item's own URI, or anything under one of the
                       roots it owns — so /case-study/vaiie-product-branding marks Case
                       Studies, and a note or a topic marks Notes. #}
                    {% set active = currentPath == item.uri %}
                    {% for root in item.owns %}
                        {% set active = active or currentPath == root or currentPath starts with root ~ '/' %}
                    {% endfor %}
                    <li class="c-nav-panel__item{% if item.mobileOnly ?? false %} c-nav-panel__item--mobile{% endif %}">
                        {# aria-current is the semantic AND the styling hook: the state is
                           announced to a screen reader and painted from the same source,
                           so a class can never drift out of step with what's read out. #}
                        {# No title attribute. It named the access key, which made it
                           discoverable — but a tooltip repeating the link's own text
                           on hover is noise, and screen readers announce title on top
                           of the link text, so every item read out twice. The keys are
                           still on the elements; they are just undiscoverable, which
                           is the normal state of affairs for accesskey. #}
                        <a
                            class="c-nav-panel__link"
                            href="{{ item.url }}"
                            accesskey="{{ item.key }}"
                            {% if active %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
                        >{{ item.label }}</a>
                    </li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </div>
    </nav>
</dialog>
